What happens after this?
Once the PROD cluster is registered:

                 GitHub
                    │
                    │ manifests
                    ▼
                Argo CD
                 /    \
                /      \
               ▼        ▼
          DEV Cluster  PROD Cluster

Argo CD can deploy applications to either cluster.
For example:

spec:
  destination:
    server: https://prod-api-server
    namespace: prod-app
Argo CD will deploy that application to the PROD cluster.

For DEV:
spec:
  destination:
    server: https://dev-api-server
    namespace: dev-app

🔥 Interview answer
If interviewer asks:
"How do you configure Argo CD to deploy applications to multiple Kubernetes clusters?"
You can say:
"First, I add each Kubernetes cluster to my kubeconfig and create separate contexts. Then I use argocd cluster add <context> to register each cluster with Argo CD. Argo CD creates the required service account and permissions on the target cluster. After registration, I can create Argo CD Applications or ApplicationSets with different destination clusters and namespaces, allowing one Argo CD instance to deploy applications across multiple Kubernetes clusters."
One important production point: the screenshot uses a service account with full cluster-admin privileges. In a real production environment, I would follow least privilege/RBAC rather than granting unnecessary cluster-wide admin access

# argocd cluster list
It displays all Kubernetes clusters currently registered with Argo CD.
Reading your output
SERVER                         NAME          VERSION   STATUS
https://1.2.3.4                admin-prod    1.21      Successful
https://kubernetes.default.svc in-cluster    1.20      Successful
You have 2 clusters registered:
Cluster	Meaning
admin-prod	External/remote PROD Kubernetes cluster
in-cluster	The Kubernetes cluster where Argo CD itself is running
admin-prod
SERVER:  https://1.2.3.4
NAME:    admin-prod
VERSION: 1.21
STATUS:  Successful
This means Argo CD can successfully communicate with the PROD cluster.

in-cluster
SERVER:  https://kubernetes.default.svc
NAME:    in-cluster
VERSION: 1.20
STATUS:  Successful

This is the cluster where Argo CD is installed.
kubernetes.default.svc is the Kubernetes API service DNS name accessible from inside the cluster.

⭐ Most important interview point
argocd cluster list is basically your verification step:
argocd cluster list
If you see:
STATUS: Successful
it means Argo CD has successfully registered and can connect to that Kubernetes cluster.
If it shows Failed, you would investigate things such as:
Service account/RBAC
Kubernetes API connectivity
credentials/certificates
network/firewall
Argo CD cluster secret
Simple flow to remember
1. kubectl config set-cluster
             ↓
2. set-credentials
             ↓
3. set-context
             ↓
4. argocd cluster add <context>
             ↓
5. argocd cluster list
             ↓
6. STATUS = Successful ✅
             ↓
7. Argo CD can deploy to that cluster
----------------------------------------------------------------------

where Argo CD stores the credentials/configuration for the registered cluster.

# kubectl describe secret cluster-1.2.3.4-1827028835 -n argocd
What is this Secret?
When you ran:
#argocd cluster add admin-prod
Argo CD created a Kubernetes Secret in the argocd namespace to store the connection information for the external cluster.
Conceptually:
argocd namespace
│
├── Argo CD components
│
└── Secret
    cluster-1.2.3.4-1827028835
         │
         ├── Server information
         ├── Authentication information
         ├── TLS information
         └── Cluster configuration
Why is this important?
Remember your previous command:
#argocd cluster list
showed:
https://1.2.3.4    admin-prod    1.21    Successful
Argo CD needs to remember how to connect to https://1.2.3.4 and authenticate to it.
That information is stored in this Secret.

What does -n argocd mean?
-n argocd
means:
Look for the Secret in the argocd namespace.
You can list these Secrets with:
kubectl get secrets -n argocd
And inspect the particular Secret:
kubectl describe secret cluster-1.2.3.4-1827028835 -n argocd
One important distinction
describe does not normally show the actual Secret values. It shows metadata and information such as:
Name
Namespace
Labels
Annotations
Type
Data

If you run:
# kubectl get secret cluster-1.2.3.4-1827028835 -n argocd -o yaml
you'll see the data fields, but Secret values are base64 encoded, not encrypted merely because they are base64.
